codex
Two serious gaps remain in the plan.

1. **The named snapshot does not produce the promised historical baseline.** The [plan](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-10-25-ingestion-smoke/plan.md:13) requires no July 24 writes. But [snapshot()](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-10-25-day-batch-review/snapshot_proof.py:30) filters by sequence alone. Reconstructing it in memory retained **31 `merged` processing rows for 30 inputs dated July 24–August 14**, because their sequence is zero. The proposed “no sequence ≥21” check therefore passes a baseline containing later writes. Require the baseline check to cover dated processing records, including sequence-zero rows. The next sequence and last completed day are otherwise correct: **21 and July 23**.

2. **The cancellation rule misses failures inside daily judging.** For example, a response with valid usage but invalid JSON passes accounting, then fails in `model.call`. [Daily judging](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:219) submits a whole level and waits for its executor to drain before propagating that failure. Requests waiting behind the two-call semaphore can still dispatch. The [planned failed-item wrapper](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-10-25-ingestion-smoke/plan.md:61) cannot catch this: `replay.run` invokes judging separately, after the item runner. Specify and prove that terminal worker failure or cancellation closes admission **before any enclosing executor drains**, including judging. This is the D173 rule already implemented by the earlier smoke’s worker wrapper.

No other serious findings in the requested scope. Review was read-only; no edits or provider calls.
